[32m+[m[32m/**[m
[32m+[m[32m * A selected or pointed-at row is a band with a DIRECTION, not a flat rectangle.[m
[32m+[m[32m *[m
[32m+[m[32m * WHY THIS SUITE EXISTS. `theme.bg("selectedBg", row)` paints one flat slab of one colour across[m
[32m+[m[32m * the whole row. Nothing in it says which end the cursor came from, so it reads as a rectangle[m
[32m+[m[32m * somebody drew rather than as a surface the cursor is resting on — the operator's verdict on the[m
[32m+[m[32m * shipped build was that the selection is "boring", and rendering the real components to PNG[m
[32m+[m[32m * confirmed the cause is that every surface is one flat ground. The replacement is a hard accent[m
[32m+[m[32m * leading cell plus an eased ramp out of `selectedBg` toward the ground behind the row.[m
[32m+[m[32m *[m
[32m+[m[32m * Four ways a directional band goes wrong, and this suite pins each:[m
[32m+[m[32m *[m
[32m+[m[32m *   1. THE WIDTH CHANGES. This is the dangerous one and it is why the width assertions come first.[m
[32m+[m[32m *      Hit-testing and layout are computed from column positions elsewhere in the product, so a[m
[32m+[m[32m *      treatment that adds or drops a single cell silently breaks mouse routing on every list.[m
[32m+[m[32m *      The band may only add zero-width escapes, and the double-width and already-coloured inputs[m
[32m+[m[32m *      are the two shapes that break a naive column walk.[m
[32m+[m[32m *   2. THE DIRECTION IS LOST. A band whose ramp runs the wrong way, or not at all, is a wash. The[m
[32m+[m[32m *      assertion decodes the `48;2;r;g;b` spans out of the returned bytes and requires each one to[m
[32m+[m[32m *      be strictly nearer the visible ground than the last, which fails on a reversed ramp, on a[m
[32m+[m[32m *      constant fill, and on an unordered splice.[m
[32m+[m[32m *   3. THE RAMP IS UNQUANTISED. A per-cell gradient is ~19 bytes of SGR per column on a row that[m
[32m+[m[32m *      repaints on every keystroke. The span count is pinned per width, so a change that goes[m
[32m+[m[32m *      per-cell (or drops to two blocks) is red rather than merely expensive.[m
